BEGIN_HYPERDESC
<h1>ORO - the rings, close up</h1>
<p>The judging rig for ORO's ring CLOSE-UP (round 2). Two DeltaGliders on orbits that lie
in Saturn's ring plane; switch with F3. <b>The effect follows the camera, not the ship</b>,
so the whole test is done with the external view: pull it out and above the ring and come
back down.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>GL-01</b> rides in the <b>Cassini Division</b> - thin, so from a few thousand
kilometres up the grooves and pebbles of the texture read against a dark band, and the
halo in the last kilometres is faint.</li>
<li><b>GL-02</b> rides just inside the outer <b>B ring</b> - dense, so the texture is
bright and the halo in the last kilometres is a real fog.</li>
</ul>
<p>What to look for, from far to near: at thousands of km the ring is exactly round 1;
below about 600 km the broad grooves come in; below ~150 km the fine ones and the first
pebbles; below ~50 km the specks; and inside five kilometres of the plane the halo. ORO
panel: WORLD -&gt; RINGS - <i>Detail</i>, <i>Particles</i> and <i>Halo</i> are each an
exact A/B at 0, and the pill is still the whole A/B. <i>Above plane</i> reads the camera's
own height.</p>
<p><i>On the orbits:</i> the elements look eccentric because Orbiter turns an ELEMENTS
line into a state vector with pure Kepler and then flies the ship in Saturn's real J2
field, so a plain "e = 0" line is NOT a circular orbit here. These are the ones that come
out circular under J2 (tools\ringplane.py derives them).</p>
END_HYPERDESC

BEGIN_ENVIRONMENT
  System Sol
  Date MJD 57897.25
END_ENVIRONMENT

BEGIN_FOCUS
  Ship GL-01
END_FOCUS

BEGIN_CAMERA
  TARGET GL-01
  MODE Extern
  POS 0.08 -12.0 25.0
  TRACKMODE TargetRelative
  FOV 50.00
END_CAMERA

BEGIN_HUD
  TYPE Orbit
END_HUD

BEGIN_MFD Left
  TYPE Orbit
  REF Saturn
END_MFD

BEGIN_MFD Right
  TYPE Map
  REF Saturn
END_MFD

BEGIN_SHIPS
